# Caddy reverse proxy — the ONLY authentication in front of MLflow (phase p4).
#
# MLflow ships with no auth of its own and holds R2 credentials it uses to
# proxy artifact reads/writes (compose.yaml's AWS_*/MLFLOW_S3_ENDPOINT_URL,
# services.mlflow.environment). An unauthenticated public MLflow is
# therefore an open door to the bucket — Caddy's basic_auth below is what
# closes it, not a convenience layer on top of something already safe.
#
# /health IS EXEMPT, DELIBERATELY. MLflow 3.15's own DNS-rebinding guard
# already exempts /health from the host allowlist (MEASURED against the
# pinned ghcr.io/mlflow/mlflow:v3.15.0 image: foreign Host -> /health 200,
# /api/... 403, / 403 — see tests/test_mlflow_allowed_hosts.py). A platform
# healthcheck (Railway's probe) cannot send credentials, so requiring
# basic_auth on /health would make the platform mark a perfectly healthy
# deploy as down. /health carries no data and never touches R2 (liveness
# only — mlflow/server/handlers.py's health route does not hit the backend
# store), so exempting it from auth costs nothing an attacker could use:
# the DNS-rebinding guard the request would need to defeat to reach
# anything else stays fully enforced one route down. Pinned by
# tests/test_caddy_auth.py (/health unauth -> 200, API unauth -> 401, both
# in the same run as the credentialed proof).
#
# THE SPLIT SHAPE MATTERS. A Caddyfile's directives are NOT ordered by
# their textual position — Caddy sorts them by a fixed internal precedence,
# so writing `handle /health { reverse_proxy }` above a bare `basic_auth`
# does NOT exempt /health: basic_auth would still apply globally regardless
# of where its block sits in the file (VERIFIED: `caddy adapt` on that
# naive shape put the `authentication` handler in `routes[0]` with no path
# matcher at all — i.e. every request, /health included). Named-matcher
# `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive (first match wins, like a switch),
# which is what actually scopes basic_auth away from /health — VERIFIED via
# `caddy adapt` against this exact file: routes[0] matches only /health and
# contains no authentication handler; routes[1] (the catch-all) contains the
# authentication handler ahead of reverse_proxy.
#
# reverse_proxy FORWARDS THE ORIGINAL Host HEADER BY DEFAULT — no directive
# overriding it to the upstream's own address appears anywhere below. VERIFIED
# against this exact pinned image (two throwaway caddy:2.11-alpine
# containers, one probe as an upstream that echoes the Host it received):
# a request sent with `Host: mlflow.entropic-substrate-lm.example` arrived
# at the upstream with that SAME Host, not `mlflow:5000`. This is why
# MLFLOW_ALLOWED_HOSTS must contain the PUBLIC domain (compose.yaml), not
# Caddy's own upstream/service name — an override here would make that
# false and 403 every real request MLflow's allowlist was configured to
# admit.
#
# CADDY_BASIC_AUTH_USER / CADDY_BASIC_AUTH_HASH arrive as runtime
# placeholders (Caddy's own `{$VAR}` syntax, resolved by Caddy's config
# loader from the CONTAINER's environment at boot) — never a literal
# anywhere in this file. `{$CADDY_BASIC_AUTH_HASH}` is a bcrypt hash
# (`caddy hash-password`, the default algorithm for this pinned Caddy
# version), which is still a credential even though it is one-way — it
# never appears in git history, only ever read from the environment. See
# compose.yaml for where those two variables come from.
:8080 {
	@health path /health
	handle @health {
		reverse_proxy mlflow:5000
	}

	handle {
		basic_auth {
			{$CADDY_BASIC_AUTH_USER} {$CADDY_BASIC_AUTH_HASH}
		}
		reverse_proxy mlflow:5000
	}
}
